e138†Chapter 7: The Income-Conscious Englishman L393: \nL394: A Victorian Mindset Finding the Right Investment Culture Trusting Only Tangible Income The Courage to Keep Going\nL395: \nL396:   * ### cite139†Chapter 8: The Frequent Value Traveler L397: \nL398: Reading between the Minds Thinking Big and Small Trouble is Opportunity Feeling the Market\nL399: \nL400:   * ### cite140†Chapter 9: The Value-Oriented Businessman L401: Learning the Numbers The Art of Contrary Thinking Targeting Good Businesses in Asia The Relativity of Valuation A Value Lifestyle\nL402: \nL403:   * ### cite141†Chapter 10: Value Investing in the Lost Decade L404: \nL405: A Musical Beginning Breaking the Language Barrier Learning from the West The Evolution of SPARX Building a Westernized Asia Searching for Value\nL406: \nL407:   * ### cite142†Chapter 11: Eternal Sunshine of the Value Mind L408: A Multidisciplinary Path Seeking a Comfortable Price Finding a Value Partner Spotting Value Minds Becoming a Man of Value\nL409: \nL410:   * ### cite143†Chapter 12: The Accidental Value Investor L411: \nL412: Starting an Investment Hobby Shop Building a Value Temple An Industrialized Process A Value March Forward\nL413: \nL414:   * ### cite144†Chapter 13: The Making of a Value Investor L415: \nL416: A Humble Portfolio Construction The Art of Valuation Reading for Ideas More than Just the Fundamentals Timing for an Exit Having the Right Temperament\nL417:   * ### cite145†Acknowledgments L418: \nL419:   * ### cite146†About the Author L420: \nL421:   * ### cite147†Index L422: \nL423: [Button: Show More]\nL424: \nL425: Content preview from cite148†The Value Investors: Lessons from the World's Top Fund Managers L426: \nL427: Preface\nL428: \nL429: Try not to become a man of success but rather to become a man of value.\nL430: \nL431: —Albert Einstein\nL432: The best advice I have ever received came from my father. When I began searching for a career after finishing college, he advised me to stay away from three potential headaches in business: labor, rent, and inventory.\nL433: As any labor-intensive business is likely to lead to office politics, my father advised that I look for a business that requires little manpower. Then, as the high office rents in a world city such as Hong Kong can easily squeeze profits, he said the ideal is to find a business that requires little office space. Finally, as handling any type of inventory requires both manpower and space, an inventory-based business should also be avoided.\nL434: Taking his advice to heart and looking for a business that met these three criteria, I found investment management to be a perfect fit. The labor force is my brain, the office need only accommodate a few desks, and the inventory is the investment positions recorded in brokerage accounts.\nL435: Although investment management may allow one to avoid the aforementioned headaches, becoming a successful investment manager still involves considerable challenges. It requires extensive knowledge of the financial markets and the ability to react to different market circumstances. It also requires a prudent strategy to achieve sustainable investment returns and a sensible investment philosophy to build the right investment model.\nL436: \nL437: In developing my own ...\nL438: ##### Become an O’Reilly member and get unlimited access to this title plus top books and audiobooks from O’Reilly and nearly 200 top publishers, thousands of courses curated by job role, 150+ live events each month,\nL439: and much more.\nL440: \nL441: cite125†Read now Unlock full access L442: ### More than 5,000 organizations count on O’Reilly\nL443: \nL444: cite149†Image: AirBnb cite150†Image: BlueOrigin cite151†Image: Electronic Arts cite152†Image: HomeDepot cite153†Image: Nasdaq cite154†Image: Rakuten cite155†Image: Tata Consultancy Services L445: \nL446: * * *\nL447: \nL448: cite156†Image: QuotationMark O’Reilly covers everything 